.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lcs
talks to the local
.BR lcsd (8)
daemon over its Unix domain socket. It does not connect directly to remote
cluster nodes.
.PP
The tool is intended for querying cluster state, performing controlled VIP
handoffs, clearing inspected conflict states, and producing simple monitoring
output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-no-colors
Disable terminal colors. By default, status and resource list use green
for healthy states, red for unavailable or failed states, and yellow for
transitional or administratively stopped states. Colors are disabled when
standard output is not a terminal, with JSON output, when TERM is dumb, or
when the NO_COLOR environment variable is nonempty.
The nrpe command always produces output without colors.
.TP
.BI \-s " SOCKET" "\fR, \fB\-\-socket " SOCKET
Connect to the given local daemon socket instead of the default
.IR /run/lcs/lcsd.sock .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the program version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage information and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Print cluster quorum, node, and VIP state as seen by the local daemon. The
cluster section includes the current membership shape duration.
.TP
.B nrpe
Print a single monitoring-plugin style status line and exit with Nagios
compatible status codes. Exit status is 0 for OK, 1 for WARNING, 2 for
CRITICAL, and 3 for UNKNOWN.
.TP
.B config status
Report resource authority, local prepared-snapshot status, canonical digests,
and prepared/matching member counts, including quorum-only members. Wire-5 and
offline peers have unknown metadata. These observations are diagnostic only;
automatic activation remains unfinished. For already-activated JSON catalogs,
also report local committed/applied/pending revisions and digests, recovery state,
admission and blocked errors. Absent pending work and errors are JSON null.
Admission does not imply current quorum or resource ownership. Supports
.BR \-\-json .
A durable pre-initialization freeze reports application_state frozen, INI
authority, no admission and no dynamic command capability. On restart it restores
the retained baseline for coordination, with resource voting, placement and
mutations fenced; later valid INI resource edits do not replace that baseline.
An explicit durable freeze blocks new acquisitions. Every member, including
quorum-only voters, must confirm its durable reload lock before owners drain.
Exact unexpired leases can renew during lock collection. Owners then drain with
the retained hooks and dependency order. Lock receipts cannot authorize
initialization. Drained freeze acknowledgements wait for cleanup and lease
state to drain; failed cleanup retains fences and appears in frozen diagnostics.
Quorum-only members perform no backend work.
Existing freezes can resume local preparation after fresh confirmation from every
member, including quorum-only voters. Pristine prepared restarts preserve history;
automatic all-member readiness then persists a barrier. Valid barriers over
revision-zero preparation survive restart without rewrites, retaining promises,
reservations and an accepted activation with its exact request identity. Missing
proof/history fails closed. Resource voting/placement
remain fenced; this does not enable JSON authority or definition commands.
Existing durable freezes automatically propose their unchanged baseline after
all-member readiness, preferring a connected full member and requiring a wire-6
majority. Timeout retries retain unknown reservations and use fresh identities
and higher ballots. Automatic first-freeze initiation from ordinary INI is pending.
A learned revision-one activation may resume initial application after restart
or hand off from a running frozen node without restarting. Existing writer and
backend work must drain; sessions, epochs and private promises are retained.
Local diagnostic format 5 reports committed revision 1 and applied revision 0
with admission disabled during baseline cleanup. Cleanup and old-owner proof
precede generation installation; fresh synchronization and quorum precede
admission. Older clients reject this new diagnostic format.
.TP
.B resource create NAME vip|service key=value...
Create one definition through majority agreement and local configuration admission.
Requires an admitted full member of an activated JSON cluster with healthy history.
.B resource update NAME key=value...
replaces fields; empty values remove optional overrides.
.B resource delete NAME
(or remove) deletes one resource without cascading through dependencies.
.TP
.B resource group create|update|add|remove|delete GROUP [options|resources]
Manage groups, memberships and type/mode policies. Group removal without resources
ungroups members and removes the definition without deleting resources.
These commands accept
.BR --request-id " ID, " --expected-revision " N and " --expected-digest " SHA256."
Omitted bases use the current committed value for new IDs and original base for
known IDs. Exact ordered argument retries return existing facts, never a second
proposal. Use the original coordinator; peer records lack client descriptors.
The CLI prints generated IDs before sending and waits up to 30 seconds for local
admission. Exit codes: 0 applied, 1 rejected, 2 committed but pending admission,
3 unknown. Usage errors retain exit 2. Supports
.BR --json .
Reasons include bound backend/projection errors and repair instructions when
available. Committed cleanup failures stay pending; uncertain storage failures
stay unknown. Fatal shutdown attempts a nonblocking diagnostic to existing waiters,
but lost delivery still leaves unknown. Historical admission is never undone by
a later failure. Retain state and query the original ID after repair/restart.
Timeout/disconnect never cancels storage or proves abort. Query request-status
with the same ID. Automatic activation remains unavailable.
.TP
.B config import FILE|-
Atomically replace all resource/group definitions from a schema-1/2 JSON document
(up to 512 KiB), using the same activated JSON full-member, quorum and request-history
gates as definition edits. Omitted definitions are deleted; static INI settings
cannot be imported. Source revision and identities are validated but the daemon
assigns the next revision and backend identities from the committed predecessor.
Accepts --request-id, --expected-revision, --expected-digest and --json, with the same
mutation outcomes/exits. Retry requires exact original bytes, including whitespace;
the local filename is not part of the identity. A partial/invalid upload cannot
reserve or propose. Upload deadline is ten seconds, followed by the ordinary
thirty-second admission wait. Input errors before submission exit 1. This command
does not activate a cluster or initialize missing request history.
New coordinated format-2 initialization requires request history before the intent
becomes prepared. Missing/corrupt history afterwards requires recovery and is never
recreated merely because no promise is visible. Format-1 catalogs retain their
legacy recovery compatibility. Automatic all-member activation remains unfinished.
.TP
.B config request-status ID
Read published local request facts from daemon memory, also available with
.BR \-\-json .
History is available, unavailable, or recovery-required. A committed request has
an exact recorded consensus decision; it does not imply resource application or
final client success. An applied request additionally records completed local
configuration admission, persisted after the handoff. It is historical completion,
not current resource health or an acknowledgement from every peer. Quorum-only
members record metadata admission, never backend execution. A reserved request's
eventual outcome is unknown. Unknown
is not rejection, abort or permission to reuse its ID. The query performs no file
I/O, peer query, initialization or proposal retry and remains available during
recovery and application transitions. IDs use 1-63 letters, digits, underscores,
dots or hyphens. Exit 0 means a valid response, including unknown; 1 indicates a
connection/daemon/protocol error; 2 indicates invalid arguments.
.TP
.B config export [committed|applied]
Write canonical resource/group JSON to stdout. The default is locally committed
definitions; applied selects definitions already applied locally. With INI authority,
both describe effective running definitions at revision zero. Export is read-only
and available during recovery or blocked application. Each response pins one
snapshot and supports bounded transfers larger than one frame. Transfer failure
returns status 1 without partial stdout; invalid syntax returns status 2.
.B \-\-json
produces the same document. Static daemon settings and recovery journals are
managed separately. Use config import for an atomic replacement.
.TP
.B config validate FILE|-
Validate resource/group JSON offline against the static context from
.B \-c CONFIG
or
.BR \-\-config " CONFIG"
(default /etc/lcs/lcs.conf). A filename of - reads stdin. Input is limited to
512 KiB. The shared strict parser checks schemas, cluster identity, field bounds,
incarnations, groups, home nodes and dependencies. No daemon connection, resource
operation or file write occurs. This establishes document validity, not live
transaction eligibility or backend/hook availability.
.B \-\-socket
is rejected; --config is supported only for this command. Exit status is 0 for
valid, 1 for validation/input/context errors and 2 for syntax errors.
.B \-\-json
reports valid, schema_version, revision, resource_count and group_count on success,
or valid=false with error on failure. Plain failures go to stderr.
.TP
.B reload
Asynchronously refresh cached peer DNS addresses and ask the local daemon to
begin a coordinated resource-configuration reload. Failed DNS lookups retain
the previous cache and do not disturb established connections.
This is equivalent to sending
.B SIGHUP
or running
.BR "systemctl reload lcsd" .
.TP
.B resource list
Print a compact resource-only view.
.TP
.BI resource " move RESOURCE NODE"
Request a controlled cluster-level move of
.I RESOURCE
to
.IR NODE .
The old owner is asked to release the VIP, the cluster records a newer lease
epoch, and the target activates the VIP only after it obtains a majority lease.
For keep-together groups, moving a lower-priority member may be redirected to
the highest-priority group member so the whole group moves consistently.
Moving a VIP away from its configured home node blocks automatic return until
a later manual move back to that home node succeeds.
.TP
.BI resource " start RESOURCE"
Clear an administrative stop and allow normal placement to start
.IR RESOURCE .
.TP
.BI resource " stop RESOURCE"
Administratively stop
.I RESOURCE
in cluster memory and release it through the normal stop path, including hooks.
The stop is not persisted across a full cluster restart.
.TP
.BI resource " clear-conflict RESOURCE"
Clear the conflict state for
.I RESOURCE
after an administrator has inspected and resolved the underlying IP conflict.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Show local cluster status:
.EX
lcs status
.EE
.TP
Query a non-default daemon socket:
.EX
lcs -s /tmp/lcs-node1.sock status
.EE
.TP
Move a VIP to another full-member node:
.EX
lcs resource move vip1 node2
.EE
.TP
Temporarily stop and start a resource:
.EX
lcs resource stop vip1
lcs resource start vip1
.EE
.TP
Run a monitoring check:
.EX
lcs nrpe
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/lcs/lcsd.sock
Default local daemon control socket.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lcsd (8)
